      "content": "that \"indirectly support\" argument feels like a bit of a reach though. when people lose their livelihoods in their hometowns, saying \"the macro economy is better\" doesn't really help them. isn't it kind of intellectually dishonest to frame a policy that actively hollows out the middle class as a \"success\" just because some GDP numbers went up? it feels like you're prioritizing abstract economic theory over the lived reality of actual people.",
